from build_registers import *
item(407,'Multipliers and rearrangement-invariant functionals','HC1-01','4,12,15,24','3–62','Early fragments concern C*-algebra multipliers, convex rearrangement-invariant functionals, and trace atoms.')
claim(20,407,'C*-algebra multipliers in the bidual','HC1-01','4','3–7','T″(xy)=T″(x)y; Γg; Γ','Bounded right-module maps on a C*-algebra C are left multiplication by unique u∈C″ with uC⊂C; adjoint-stable two-sided idealizers give the multiplier algebra. Hermitian left multipliers are two-sided.','Use the unit of the von Neumann bidual and separate weak-star continuity. The wording translations gauches conflicts with the displayed right-module identity; preserve the identity as the precise mathematical version. For an ambient idealizer the induced homomorphism need not be injective without essentiality.','fragment, ending mid-proof')
claim(21,407,'Pólya functional representation','HC1-01','12,15','14–43','P symétrique, convexe; semi-continuité','On a finite-measure support, a convex rearrangement-invariant functional with the stated bounded convergence-in-measure lower semicontinuity is weak-star lower semicontinuous and is a supremum of affine rearrangement pairings.','Convex sublevels closed under bounded convergence in measure are weak-star closed by bounded-set closure and Krein–Šmulian. Hahn–Banach plus rearrangement gives the support representation. The transcription alternates weak and weak-star topologies; σ(L∞,L1) is the topology proved.','argument continuing across nonconsecutive scans')
claim(22,407,'Atoms of a trace and minimal projections','HC1-01','24','45–62','trace non atomique; projecteur minimal','The fragment relates atoms in a maximal abelian subalgebra to minimal projections, and sketches diffuse trace reduction.','The ambient algebra b, trace faithfulness, and availability of characteristic projections are not defined on the supplied fragment. In a von Neumann algebra with faithful normal trace, an atomic projection of a MASA is minimal in the full algebra: a nontrivial corner projection would commute with the MASA and violate maximality. A point atom of a measure on a C*-spectrum alone need not give a continuous characteristic projection.','heavily revised incomplete fragment')
item(408,'Singular-value minimax and Hilbert tensors','HC1-01;K1-01','27,35,39,49,51','HC1-01:65–261; K1-01:7–48','Singular values, elementary ideals, and Hilbert tensor identities supply the later operator theory.')
claim(23,408,'Trace minimax and generalized singular values','HC1-01','27,35','66–151','Inf Tr E<t Sup (Ax,x)','The trace analogue of minimax expresses a decreasing rearrangement of a positive operator through complementary projections of bounded trace.','The displayed supremum omits ||x||≤1; literally it is infinite for a nonzero positive A on a nonzero complement. The surrounding norm equality identifies the intended unit-ball formulation. Strict versus non-strict trace bounds determine one-sided continuity at atoms; the diffuse case removes that boundary issue.')
claim(24,408,'Ideal, order, adjoint, and continuity inequalities','HC1-01','27,35','92–169','φ|BA|≤||B||φ|A|; φ|1+A|≤1+φ|A|','Singular-value functions satisfy ideal inequalities, monotonicity for positive operators, equality for A and A*, and Lipschitz continuity in operator norm; adding the identity gives μt(I+A)≤1+μt(A).','Minimax proves each inequality, and polar decomposition gives equality of nonzero spectral distributions. Missing unit-ball restriction is inherited only by the preceding display, not by the norm formulas.')
claim(25,408,'Individual Ky Fan inequalities','HC1-01','39','175–231','ρm+n+1(AB)≤ρm+1(A)ρn+1(B)','For compact Hilbert-space operators, s_(m+n-1)(AB)≤s_m(A)s_n(B) and the analogous sum bound hold.','The scan39 indices are uncertain. Cote1/144 supplies a clear one-based statement and a proof whose codimension signs require correction; merge the mathematical subject without changing either reading.','question-marked recalled inequalities')
claim(26,408,'Hilbert tensor and L2 identities','HC1-01','49','234–261','ei⊗fj base orthonormale','Hilbert tensor products have tensor orthonormal bases, cross norm on pure tensors, and conjugation compatibility; K1-01/51 identifies L²(μ)⊗₂E with Bochner L²_E and gives ℓ² and product-measure instances.','The basis construction and density argument are valid. Product-measure and vector-valued L² use the usual Radon/strong-measurability conventions; no claim is made for arbitrary weakly measurable functions.','proof with continuation K1-01:7–48')
item(409,'Hilbert–Schmidt and trace-class operators','K1-01','51–58','51–448','The historical term opérateur de Fredholm here means a nuclear/trace-class operator, not a modern Fredholm operator.')
claim(27,409,'Hilbert–Schmidt characterization and ideal property','K1-01','51–55','51–234','Ebar⊗₂F=L²(E,F)','Hilbert–Schmidt operators are the Hilbert tensor product, have squared norm Σ||ue_i||²=Σs_i², are compact, and satisfy ||Au||₂≤||A||||u||₂ and adjoint invariance.','The source proof is complete after adding compactness to the spectral characterization, as the proof itself does. A bounded operator with no eigenvalues is not tested merely by an empty eigenvalue sum.')
claim(28,409,'Projective tensor product and trace-class SVD','K1-01','55–56','241–347','||u||1=Tr|u|=Σρi','The projective tensor map E′⊗πF is injective for Hilbert spaces and identifies with S1; the trace norm equals the singular-value sum and is dual to bounded operators via trace.','The singular-value expansion and finite-rank compressions prove the norm formula. The marginal Non! has no unambiguous target in the supplied text; it is not evidence that the whole theorem is false.')
claim(29,409,'Square root coefficient and zero eigenspaces','K1-01','56','290–303','sqrt(uu*)=Σρi² bi⊗bi','The expansion of sqrt(uu*) should have coefficient ρi, not ρi². Equality of spectral lists of |u| and |u*| concerns nonzero eigenvalues with multiplicity.','The literal squared coefficient fails for the scalar u=2. Equality of full eigenspaces at zero can fail on infinite Hilbert space, as a compact weighted unilateral shift shows. This distinction returns at scan128.')
claim(30,409,'Trace Hölder, Hilbert duality, and factorization','K1-01','56–58','354–448','||vu||1≤||v||2||u||2','Products of two S2 operators lie in S1; trace realizes the S2 pairing; u is S2 iff vu is S1 for every S2 v; every S1 operator factors through two S2 factors with equal norm √||u||1.','The finite-rank trace proof, closed graph argument, and polar square-root factorization are valid. The editorial request to delete or demote the first corollary does not retract its mathematics.')
item(410,'Hilbert exterior powers','K1-01;K1-02','59–70','K1-01:454–758; K1-02:4–144','Exterior powers transfer singular-value and eigenvalue inequalities to operator norms.')
claim(31,410,'Exterior Hilbert norms and normalization','K1-01','59–64','454–758','||a1∧…∧an||²=det((ai,aj))','Antisymmetrization defines the Hilbert exterior power with Gram determinant norm; wedge bases are orthonormal, Hadamard follows, and the mixed exterior operator norm is at most the product of norms.','The theorem is valid. At64 the factors attributed to the normalized inclusion and quotient are reversed: for a_n=(1/n!)Alt, its norm on wedge space is 1/√n! and the quotient has norm √n!. Correct rescalings are √n! a_n and φ_n/√n!; their product cancels and the final bound survives.')
claim(32,410,'Exterior functoriality and functional calculus','K1-02','65–67','4–52','Λn(vu)=Λnv Λnu; (Λnh)^α=Λn(h^α)','Exterior powers preserve multiplication, adjoints and positivity; for bounded positive h, positive powers commute with Λn.','The rational-root argument plus norm continuity of positive powers for α>0 proves the assertion; no value α=0 is required.')
claim(33,410,'Spectrum of exterior powers and its continuity','K1-02','67–69','65–91','λi1…λin; dans un sens évident à préciser','For compact u, the nonzero eigenvalues of Λn u, with algebraic multiplicity, are the products of n distinct indexed eigenvalues of u. Finite-rank approximation is justified by spectral cluster continuity away from zero.','The finite-rank block reduction is correct. The source leaves the topology of whole spectral systems unspecified; Riesz projections and finite nonzero clusters give a precise proof, with arbitrarily small eigenvalues handled at zero.','finite-rank proof plus explicit continuity placeholder')
claim(34,410,'Weyl and product log-majorization','K1-02','69–70','95–144','|λ1…λn|≤ρ1…ρn','The product of the n largest eigenvalue moduli is bounded by the first n singular values; s(uv) is weakly log-majorized by s(u)s(v).','These follow from spectral radius≤norm and multiplicativity of Λn, with ||Λn u||=∏_(j≤n)s_j(u). Composition order uv is occasionally mistyped for v∘u; domain/codomain types fix the stated version. The page expressly credits Weyl.')
item(411,'Fredholm determinant and symmetric coefficients','K1-02;K1-03;K1-06;K1-07','71–80,124–126,149–150','K1-02:147–397,480–545; K1-03:4–63; K1-06:50–228; K1-07:383–437','Mixed exterior trace estimates underpin determinant inequalities, product formulas, and resolvent bounds.')
claim(35,411,'Mixed exterior trace bounds','K1-02','71,77–78','149–199,480–545','||u1∧…∧un||1≤(1/n!)∏||ui||1','Mixed exterior products of S1 maps have trace norm bounded by 1/n! times the product of trace norms; αn is their trace, ||Λn u||1=e_n(s(u)), and ||Λn u||₂²=e_n(s(u)²).','The rank-one determinant calculation and multilinear extension prove the constants. Some later displays drop norm subscripts or confuse S1 and S2; the explicit proof identifies the correct norm. The marginal request vérifier d’après cas décomposable is resolved by this calculation.')
claim(36,411,'Determinant domination and a literal equality error','K1-02','71','212–216','|det(1+u)|=det(1+|u|)','The literal equality is false; the correct general bound is |det(I+zu)|≤det(I+|z||u|), explicitly stated at K1-03/79.','Scalar u=−1 gives 0 versus2. This is a direct disagreement between supplied leaves, not a conjectured emendation. The coefficient domination proof supports the inequality.')
claim(37,411,'Trace equals spectral sum and genus-zero determinant','K1-02','71','218–241','Tr u=Σλi; det(1+zu)=∏(1+zλi)','For S1 operators on Hilbert space the eigenvalues are absolutely summable, the trace equals their sum, and the Fredholm determinant is their genus-zero product.','The result is valid (Lidskii). This leaf announces reduction to the positive case but does not supply it. Deleted drafts K1-03/79–80 and later entire-function lemmas explain the intended analytic route; do not treat the positive case alone as proof for nonnormal u.','announced theorem with incomplete proof here')
claim(38,411,'Determinant and coefficient subadditivity','K1-02','71–73','243–386','αn(|u+v|)≤Σαp(|u|)αn-p(|v|)','The mixed p,q exterior estimate has factor p!q!/(p+q)! and gives coefficientwise domination, hence det(I+r|u+v|)≤det(I+r|u|)det(I+r|v|).','Expanding Λn(u+v), applying the mixed estimate, and cancelling binomial factors proves this. The finite-truncation version follows from rank-n contractions; it does not assert optimality among all possible singular-value constraints.')
claim(39,411,'Variational formulas for truncated symmetric sums','K1-02','73–74','352–397','σn^m(u)=Sup |αm(vnu)|','The elementary symmetric polynomial of the first n singular values is the supremum of |αm(vu)| over rank≤n contractions v; the analogous product ∏_(i≤n)(1+s_i) is a determinant supremum.','Choose v mapping the first left singular vectors to the right singular vectors to attain the bound. The determinant supremum display lacks absolute value or real-part restriction, so is not ordered on complex determinants as printed.')
claim(40,411,'Claimed optimality of the truncated determinant inequalities','K1-02','74','389','semblent les inégalités les plus fortes possibles','The draft asks whether its truncated elementary-symmetric and determinant bounds are the strongest possible constraints on s(u+v) from s(u),s(v).','Not established by the displayed proofs. Complete singular-value sum constraints involve Horn-type inequalities for arbitrary index sets; precise comparison must distinguish completeness of a feasible-spectrum description from sharpness of one bound.','explicit tentative optimality assertion')
item(412,'Subadditive singular-value functions','K1-02','75–76','405–468','The logarithmic determinant bound is integrated to fractional-power trace inequalities and a broad classification question.')
claim(41,412,'Integral representation and p-subadditivity','K1-02','75–76','405–460','S_p^(n)(u+v)≤S_p^(n)(u)+S_p^(n)(v)','Integrating log(1+rs) subadditivity against positive measures yields sums of functions f(s)=∫φ(r)s/(1+rs)dr for nonnegative decreasing φ; φ(r)=r^(−p),0<p<1 gives s^p up to π/sinπp.','Integration by parts is valid with the stated boundary/positivity conditions. At75 the derivative is printed r/(1+rρ) instead of ρ/(1+rρ); the preceding integral formula and later p-calculation use the correct derivative.')
claim(42,412,'A stronger p-power comparison is false','K1-02','76','462','Σs_i(u+v)^p≤Σ(s_i(u)+s_i(v))^p','The extra remark asserts weak-majorization after applying a concave power to aligned singular-value sums.','For u=diag(1,0),v=diag(0,1),n=2,0<p<1, the left side is2 and the right side2^p<2. The valid separate-sums inequality in claim041 is unaffected.')
claim(43,412,'Classifying subadditive spectral functions','K1-02','76','466–468','Trouver la forme générale des fonctions f','Determine f on the positive half-line for which every Ky Fan partial sum of f(s(u+v)) is bounded by the corresponding sums for u and v; the leaf also asks for multivariable extensions.','Nonnegative concave functions form a substantial established sufficient class. The manuscript imposes no regularity, normalization at0, or monotonicity, so it does not specify one unique modern classification problem. Necessity and the unrestricted multivariable request are not settled merely by citing the concave theorem.','explicit question')
item(413,'Convexity and majorization','K1-03;K1-04','81–93','K1-03:68–494; K1-04:4–61','Three convexity theorems derive spectral, product, and sum inequalities; two finite-dimensional variants omit symmetry.')
claim(44,413,'Convex symmetric functions and majorization','K1-03','81–87','68–255','φ(α)≤φ(β)','Partial-sum domination of a decreasing α by β implies comparison for symmetric increasing convex functions; equal total sum permits dropping increasing. The multiplicative version follows by logarithms, with limits handling zero coordinates.','Supporting affine functions, rearrangement, and summation by parts give a correct proof. The β ordering is not explicitly required by this stronger ordered-partial-sum assumption. Zero-coordinate approximation in the revised proof is valid.')
claim(45,413,'Reciprocal log-majorization has a reversed display','K1-03','85','197–209','f(1/σ)≤f(1/ρ)','Under equal total product and weak log-majorization ρ≺logσ, the reciprocal comparison has the same direction f(1/ρ)≤f(1/σ) for symmetric log-convex f.','Literal displayed reverse fails for ρ=(1,1),σ=(2,1/2),f(x)=x1+x2:2.5≤2 is false. The proof reverses the original partial-product hypothesis midway; this is an actual logical error on the leaf.')
claim(46,413,'First convexity theorem and spectral p-sums','K1-03','87–91','260–381','Σ|λi|^p≤Σρi^p','Weyl log-majorization gives every symmetric increasing log-convex comparison, including partial p-sums for all p>0 and symmetric gauge norms.','Valid consequence of claim034 and044. A display identifies Σs_i^p with ||u||p rather than ||u||p^p; later norms are defined with the pth root, so this is a numerical notation error.')
claim(47,413,'Finite eigenvalue comparison omits symmetry','K1-03','89–90','335–357','f(e^t) convexe (pas forcément croissante)','For invertible finite matrices equality of determinant moduli permits symmetric log-convex f without coordinatewise monotonicity.','The source omits symmetric in this particular statement. Without it the claim is false: take u=[[1,1],[0,1]] and f(s1,s2)=s2. Eigenvalue input gives1, while the second singular value is<1. The corrected symmetric theorem follows directly from equal-product majorization.')
claim(48,413,'Product convexity and Schatten Hölder','K1-03','91–92','383–463','||uv||r≤||u||p||v||q','Product log-majorization yields symmetric log-convex comparisons and Hölder for all positive p,q,r with1/r=1/p+1/q, including quasi-norms below1. Trace pairing is bounded by Σs_i(u)s_i(v).','The proof reduces to scalar Hölder exponents p/r,q/r≥1. The text says f≡1 for the linear sum corollary; f(s)=s is needed. Zero exponents in one display are inadmissible for the reciprocal formulas.')
claim(49,413,'Finite product comparison omits symmetry','K1-03','92','469–490','f…sym deleted; formula39','The equal-product finite-dimensional comparison still requires a symmetric f even when u,v are invertible.','For u=diag(2,1),v=diag(1,2),f(s)=s2, the claimed left side is2 and the right side1. The printed deletion of symmetry cannot be accepted as a valid strengthening.')
claim(50,413,'Sum convexity and symmetric gauge triangle inequality','K1-04','93','4–61','N(s(u+v))≤N(s(u))+N(s(v))','Ky Fan partial-sum majorization gives comparison for symmetric increasing convex functions of singular values, and the triangle inequality for every symmetric gauge norm, including Schatten p≥1.','The argument correctly needs ordinary convexity, which the revised statement has; log-convexity alone would not justify sums. The proof is sound once the cited Ky Fan sum inequality is supplied.')
item(414,'Symmetric sequence spaces and operator ideals','K1-04','94–105','66–517','The draft carefully distinguishes maximal sequence/ideal spaces from closures of finite-support or finite-rank elements.')
claim(51,414,'Minimal and maximal symmetric sequence spaces','K1-04','94–97','74–198','ℓN closure of finite sequences; ℓ^N finite extended norm','A symmetric gauge norm extends by monotone finite truncations, defining a Banach space ℓ^N and its finite-support closure ℓN. One has ℓ1⊂ℓ^N⊂ℓ∞ and ℓ1⊂ℓN⊂c0; ℓ^N⊂c0 unless N is equivalent to sup norm.','Finite top-n gauge norms extend any finite-dimensional gauge. Completeness follows by summable series or polar duality. The deleted identity ℓN=ℓ^N∩c0 is not adopted; it fails for non-order-continuous symmetric spaces.')
claim(52,414,'Norm-order inclusions are reversed in the first draft','K1-04','96–97','144,184','N′≤N″ ⇒ ℓ^N′⊂ℓ^N″','The correct inclusion is ℓ^N″⊂ℓ^N′ and likewise for finite-support completions.','Taking N′=sup,N″=ℓ1 gives a direct contradiction to the printed inclusion, e.g. (1/n)∈c0 but notℓ1. The later operator summary at102:380 states the correct direction, so internal evidence corroborates the alarm.')
claim(53,414,'Polar duality and reflexivity of sequence spaces','K1-04','97–98','200–231','(N°)°=N; (ℓN)′=ℓ^(N°)','Polar gauge duality identifies (ℓN)* with ℓ^(N°), and reflexivity is equivalent to equality of minimal/maximal spaces for both N and its polar.','Finite truncations prove bipolarity and the dual representation. Closed plus weakly total implies equality in a reflexive space, explaining the question-marked step. The general dual of ℓ^N can be larger, as the text correctly notes.')
claim(54,414,'Summary slips in polar and operator duality','K1-04','97,102–103','174,358,412','(N°)°=N°; dual LN=LN','Three shorthand lines lose a polar or reverse space: ℓ^N=(ℓN)*, (N°)°=N°, and (LN(E,F))*=L^N(E,F).','All fail in general; the full nearby proofs give (ℓ_(N°))*=ℓ^N, (N°)°=N, and (LN(E,F))*=L^(N°)(F,E). With N=ℓ1 the polar is sup, making the error explicit.')
claim(55,414,'Symmetric operator ideals and their duality','K1-04','98–105','233–518','(LN(E,F))′=L^(N°)(F,E)','The finite-rank closure LN is dual to maximal L^(N°) under trace; the maximal norm can be defined by finite-rank trace testing on all bounded operators. It is complete, is adjoint invariant and an ideal, and its reflexivity matches the sequence-space criterion when both Hilbert spaces are infinite dimensional.','The compactness argument uses a spectral projection of infinite rank if |v| is not compact. Finite-rank diagonal tests force the excluded constant sequence into the polar space. Finite-dimensional compressions settle the bounded-operator endpoint; the final full theorem restores the correct polar and variance.')
claim(56,414,'Three-gauge multiplication needs both gauges in its hypothesis','K1-04','99–104','267–287,420,469–490','N(xy)≤N′(x)N′(y)','A scalar multiplication estimate N(xy)≤N′(x)N″(y) transfers to the corresponding operator ideals by log-majorization and finite-rank compression.','The first full statement is correct; later summaries repeat N′ on both factors while drawing a conclusion with arbitrary N″. Literal summary is false: N=ℓ1,N′=ℓ2,N″=sup and u=v=I2 give2≤√2. Domain/codomain and factor labels also require consistency.')
claim(57,414,'Unitary invariance characterizes symmetric gauge norms','K1-04','101','323–336','p(Vu)=p(u)=p(uU)','A norm on finite-rank Hilbert-space operators invariant under left/right unitaries and normalized on rank-one tensors is exactly a symmetric gauge function of singular values.','SVD reduces to diagonal finite sequences; permutations and phases give gauge symmetry. This characterizes the norm, while maximal and minimal completions remain separate constructions.')
item(415,'Entire functions: genus, order, and zero counting','K1-04;K1-05','107–119','K1-04:519–541; K1-05:4–534','Classical entire-function tools are developed for determinant and spectral estimates.')
claim(58,415,'Weierstrass, Hadamard, genus and order','K1-04','107','519–541','Décomposition en facteurs; Weierstrass','Entire functions admit canonical products; finite order ρ permits a polynomial exponential factor and genus g with g≤ρ≤g+1. Coefficient decay controls order.','The continuation K1-05/108–110 gives these standard statements. It swaps the names of ω,g in prose while the boxed inequality treats ω as order and g as genus. Bounds exp(|z|^k) need large |z| or a multiplicative constant. These notation defects do not overturn Hadamard factorization.')
claim(59,415,'The product with zeros n^α has order 1/α','K1-05','110,114','108–135,304–342','∏(1−z/n^α)…d’ordre1(sauf erreur)','For α>1, the genus-zero canonical product with zeros n^α has order1/α.','The explicit order1 remark is false. The same dossier later proves the correct reciprocal order at114, with a head/tail product bound. Zero counting gives the lower bound. This is an internal correction, not a newly claimed theorem.','explicit sauf erreur; later contrary computation')
claim(60,415,'Genus-zero domination lemma','K1-05','111–112','139–249','coefficients majorés…genre zéro','If coefficients of f are dominated in modulus by those of a genus-zero entire function and the reciprocals of nonzero zeros of f are summable, then f has genus zero.','Hadamard gives f=e^(a+bz) times a genus-zero product; minimal exponential type forces b=0. The supplied rotational proof works with π/3<θ<π/2 and the conjugate rotation bbar/|b|. The page writes θ<π and b/|b|, both too broad/wrong. Coefficient domination gives |f(z)|≤h(|z|) after choosing a positive-coefficient majorant, not |h(z)| everywhere.')
claim(61,415,'Sequence order and a limsup/liminf error','K1-05','113','253–300','1/ρ=limsup log(1/αn)/log n','For a decreasing null sequence, the infimum of summability powers equals the infimum of polynomial-decay exponents, and summability of αn^s implies αn=o(n^(−1/s)).','The little-o statement and equality of thresholds are valid. The reciprocal logarithmic formula needs liminf, not limsup. Plateau sequence αn=N_k^(−1) for N_(k−1)<n≤N_k, N_k=2^(2^k), has thresholdρ=1 but displayed limsup=2.')
claim(62,415,'Canonical product order and proposed vector-valued orders','K1-05','114–115','304–391','ordre de la suite; ordre d’une partie bornée','For a summable sequence α, the canonical product ∏(1+αnz) has order equal to its exponent of convergence; replacing complex α by |α| preserves that order. The page proposes strong/absolute summability orders for vector-valued sequences and convex-hull orders for bounded sets.','The scalar result follows from zero-counting and the head/tail bound. The display calls an unmodified absolutely convergent product genre1, although the argument is for genus0. The vector and set definitions are sketches without fixed ambient topology or a claimed theorem.','proved scalar result, followed by prospective definitions')
claim(63,415,'Counting-function integrals and Jensen estimates','K1-05','116–119','395–534','Σλ(rν)=boundary+∫n(r)(−λ′(r))dr','Stieltjes integration by parts relates zero counts to reciprocal powers and decreasing weights. Jensen’s formula then relates the count to angular means of log|f|.','The real-part formula is valid with f(0)≠0 and endpoint conventions. The tail power display omits exponent α. Using a principal complex logarithm globally without jump terms is not justified; taking log|f| from the start repairs the derivation. Under |f(0)|=1 Jensen gives0≤V(r)≤log M(r); without normalization the stated |V|≤M can fail for a small constant function.')
item(416,'A holomorphic function on ℓ1 with finite uniform radius','K1-05;K1-06','120–122','K1-05:537–643; K1-06:3–48','S(λ)=Σn!e_n(λ) converges at every λ, is locally holomorphic everywhere, and is unbounded on the unit ball.')
claim(64,416,'Coefficient decay for genus-zero functions','K1-05','120–121','540–643','sqrt[n](n!|αn|)→0','For λ∈ℓ1, elementary symmetric coefficients satisfy (n!|e_n(λ)|)^(1/n)→0, so Σn!e_n(zλ) converges for every scalar z.','Minimal exponential type and the ordinary Cauchy bound |a_n|≤M(R)/R^n prove the result. The draft prints R^(n+1), and its following e^n factor has an index mismatch. Scalar f(z)=1+z,R=10 disproves the literal Cauchy display; the theorem survives with the standard bound.')
claim(65,416,'Everywhere holomorphic versus bounded type on ℓ1','K1-05','120','561–590','pas bornée dans la boule unité…pas une fonction entière','S(λ)=Σn!e_n(λ) is everywhere Fréchet holomorphic, but unbounded on the unit ball; its Taylor series at0 has uniform norm radius1. K1-06/122 proves unboundedness using m coordinates1/m.','The unboundedness and radius calculation are valid. The implication not bounded on the unit ball ⇒ not entire is false in modern Banach-holomorphic terminology; it means not entire of bounded type. Local holomorphy at each λ follows by splitting a finite head from a tail of ℓ1-norm<1 and estimating the tail series. K1-06’s margin itself says S is holomorphic.')
item(417,'Mixed determinants and full Schatten theory','K1-06','124–136','50–662','A second route proves determinant subadditivity and Schatten Hölder through analytic integral formulas.')
claim(66,417,'Mixed exterior Cauchy–Schwarz inequality','K1-06','124','50–100','||u1∧…∧un||1²≤αn(|ui|)αn(|ui*|)','Mixed exterior trace norm squared is bounded by the product of mixed positive coefficients on domain and range; arithmetic mean gives the subsequent bound.','The explicit SVD expansion and scalar Cauchy–Schwarz prove this. The opening formula is damaged, but the two squared Gram-determinant sums in the proof identify the precise claim.')
claim(67,417,'One-sided mixed coefficient domination is false','K1-06','124','57','|αn(u1,…,un)|≤αn(|u1|,…,|un|)','The separate one-sided mixed inequality is stronger than the two-sided Cauchy–Schwarz estimate.','False for n=2: u1=[[1,0],[0,0]],u2=[[0,0],[3/5,4/5]]. Mixed determinant coefficient is2/5, while that of |u1|,|u2| is8/25. The valid geometric-mean bound includes the range-side factor1/2.')
claim(68,417,'Determinant subadditivity via positive operators','K1-06','124–126','102–228','det(1+|A+B|)≤det(1+|A|)det(1+|B|)','Mixed Cauchy–Schwarz reduces determinant subadditivity to positive h,k; det(I+h+k)=det(I+h)det(I+(I+h)^(−1)k) proves that case. A polar contraction gives the |A+B| version.','The conclusion is valid. The proof says the polar factor is unitary in general, false for a unilateral weighted shift; a partial isometry/contraction suffices. For unitary U, |UA|=|A|, not U|A|U^(−1) as printed; either valid equality of singular values yields the determinant step.')
claim(69,417,'Schatten quasi-Banach spaces','K1-06','127–129','235–350','Sp(A+B)≤Sp(A)+Sp(B),0<p≤1','S_p is a complete normed ideal for p≥1 and a complete p-quasi-normed ideal for0<p<1; it is not locally convex in infinite dimension for p<1. Adjoint/ideal invariance, spectral p-sum bounds, and Hölder hold.','The topology assertions follow from finite-rank approximation and the diagonal ℓp subspace. The displayed p-range before the p-triangle sentence briefly says∞, but formula4 explicitly restricts p≤1. The general gauge extension requested in the margin is supplied in K1-04.')
claim(70,417,'Absolute values need not be unitarily equivalent','K1-06','128','296–299','|A| et |A*| unitairement équivalents(?)','Compact |A| and |A*| have the same nonzero eigenvalues and hence the same Schatten quantities, but need not be unitarily equivalent.','Take A=S diag(1/n) on ℓ²: |A| is injective, |A*| has one-dimensional kernel. This answers the uncertain (?) precisely without deciding whether that glyph is a question mark or a reference.')
claim(71,417,'Fractional power trace integral','K1-06','129–130','352–391','Σαi^p=(p sinπp/π)∫log f(r)r^(−1−p)dr','For0<p<1 and nonnegative α, monotone convergence gives the displayed integral identity, allowing∞; determinant subadditivity implies Schatten p-subadditivity.','For one scalar, integration by parts and the beta integral give the exact constant; summing nonnegative terms is justified. A divergent product may be treated as an extended-valued limit rather than an entire function.')
claim(72,417,'Analytic proof of Weyl p-sum inequality','K1-06','130–132','395–456','sp(A)≤(pπ/sinπp)Sp(A); use A^n','A Jensen estimate first gives a constant>1 for p<1; applying it to A^n with exponent p/n and using Hölder lets the constant tend to1 for every p>0.','The strategy is valid provided Hölder is proved independently, as the source promises and later does. It does not prove Hölder by using the very spectral bound it is deriving; the exterior/dyadic route supplies an independent argument.')
claim(73,417,'Dyadic trace Hölder and trace-algebra extension','K1-06','133–135','485–615','2^n facteurs; trace von Neumann–Dixmier','Repeated Hilbert–Schmidt Cauchy–Schwarz gives trace Hölder for dyadic numbers of factors, then dyadic exponent approximation gives conjugate Hölder and multifactor trace estimates. The draft proposes extension to a semifinite trace algebra.','The cyclic trace rearrangement is correct. Finite-rank approximation justifies exponent limits without integrability trouble; the noncommutative trace extension needs a faithful normal semifinite trace and measurable-operator framework, not merely a symbol τ on an arbitrary C*-algebra.','proof plus explicit ?? extension and missing-reference requests')
claim(74,417,'Exterior coefficient product estimate','K1-06','136','617–662','αn(L^m)≤αn(H^m)αn(K^m)','For A,B∈S_(2m),H=A*A,K=B*B,L=(AB)*(AB), one has e_n(L^m)≤e_n(H^m)e_n(K^m).','Apply Λn and reduce to n=1. Cyclically rearrange the trace of (ABB*A*)^m and use the independently proved multifactor Hölder plus monotonicity of Schatten exponents. The extra absolute signs on positive L are harmless.')
item(418,'Analytic Hölder completed','K1-07','137–140','3–150','The determinant coefficient comparison gives a weighted Young inequality and then multiplicative Hölder.')
claim(75,418,'Generating-function proof and optimization','K1-07','137–140','3–150','Sr(AB)≤(r/p)Sp(A)+(r/q)Sq(B)','With1/r=1/p+1/q, positive generating-function coefficient comparison and the fractional-power integral yield a sine-ratio estimate; m→∞ gives weighted Young. Scaling A,B and optimizing gives ||AB||r≤||A||p||B||q for all positive exponents.','Take m strictly larger than max(p,q)/2 to keep the integral exponents inside(0,1). The minimization equals a^(r/p)b^(r/q); zero cases follow by limit. The final strict c< is wrong at equality (scalar A=B=1); it must be≤. The two requested lemmas are thus fully reconstructed.')
item(419,'Convergence, convexity, and fractional powers','K1-07','142–148','154–381','Spectral cluster convergence, Schatten convergence, power-map continuity, and explicit convexity questions occur together.')
claim(76,419,'Spectral criterion for Schatten convergence','K1-07','142,145','154–174,265–278','||Hi||p→||H0||p; convergence spectrale','For compact self-adjoint operators and0<p<∞, convergence of each nonzero spectral cluster with multiplicities/projections, plus p-norm convergence, gives S_p convergence. Operator-norm convergence plus p-norm convergence is an equivalent convenient criterion.','The first statement says every spectral λ but the isolation condition excludes0 when0 is an accumulation point; scan145 explicitly restricts λ≠0. Equality of topologies for finite clusters requires an actual finite-dimensional identification. The nonselfadjoint analogue cannot use eigenvalues alone, because nilpotent parts are invisible.')
claim(77,419,'Continuity of absolute and positive power maps','K1-07','142–146','176–181,274–298','A↦|A|^r : Lp→L(p/r)','For all p,r>0 the absolute-power map is continuous between the corresponding Schatten quasi-norm spaces. On all bounded positive operators, t↦t^r functional calculus is norm continuous on bounded spectral intervals.','For bounded positive operators polynomial approximation proves continuity for every r>0, answering the explicit sans doute question. Quantitative Hölder inequalities are stronger and must not be inferred solely from continuity.')
claim(78,419,'Weak convergence, norm convergence, and uniform convexity','K1-07','143,145–146','183–188,278–282','Est-ce vrai si p<∞? Uniforme convexité?','The draft asks whether weak convergence plus p-norm convergence implies S_p convergence beyond p≤2, and whether S_p is uniformly convex for0<p<∞.','For1<p<∞ uniform convexity gives the Radon–Riesz property; WOT convergence plus bounded S_p norms implies weak S_p convergence by finite-rank density in the dual. S1 is not uniformly convex, and0<p<1 has no norm with this quasi-norm’s nonconvex unit ball. The precise meaning of faible at p≤1 requires a separate Kadec–Klee comparison, not uniform convexity.')
claim(79,419,'Operator-norm fractional-power Hölder bound','K1-07','143,146','190–196,284–298','||H^r−K^r||^(1/r)≤||H−K||','For bounded positive H,K and0<r<1, ||H^r−K^r||≤||H−K||^r.','This precise proposed inequality is established; an operator-monotone comparison proves it from H≤K+δI and the scalar subadditivity of x^r. It answers norm continuity quantitatively. The endpoint r=0 in one draft display is not meaningful as written.')
claim(80,419,'Schatten monotonicity in the power parameter','K1-07','143,146','190–196,290–298','Sp(|H^r−K^r|^(1/r)) croissante en r','For fixed positive H,K and p>0, the draft asks whether r↦Tr|H^r−K^r|^(p/r) is increasing, and suggests equivalence with the operator-norm Hölder bound.','This is stronger than the operator-norm bound, which controls only the largest singular value. The inference of equivalence is unjustified. Exact range in p and r requires a separate trace/matrix comparison and is under targeted investigation.','explicit conjectural monotonicity question')
claim(81,419,'Eigenvalue multiset continuity in S_p','K1-07','147–148','301–381','Ai→A in Lp ⇒ Âi→Â in Σp','S_p convergence implies convergence of eigenvalue multisets in the p-summable matching topology, using finite nonzero spectral clusters and uniform small-eigenvalue p-tails.','The convex truncated-exponential majorant is appropriate. The proof wrongly claims ∫₀∞n(r)/r dr finite; it already diverges for one nonzero eigenvalue. The needed weighted limit ρ^p∫ρ∞n(r)/r dr→0 follows instead by dominated convergence term by term against the summable p-powers. Σp itself is not defined on these leaves, so its matching convention is a present reconstruction.')
item(420,'Individual eigenvalue bounds and Fredholm minors','K1-07','144,149–150','201–263,383–437','The closing leaves ask for individual eigenvalue comparisons and estimate normal Fredholm minors.')
claim(82,420,'Ky Fan proof has reversed codimension inequalities','K1-07','144','201–258','codim(H∩E)≥codimH+codimE','The sum and product singular-value inequalities are valid with codim(H∩E)≤codimH+codimE≤m+n−2.','The printed ≥ signs are false and point the wrong way for the final monotonicity step. Taking identical hyperplanes gives codim1<2. Correcting the inequalities reconstructs the elementary minimax proof; no source text is silently changed.')
claim(83,420,'Individual eigenvalues versus singular values','K1-07','144','260–262','limsup |λi|/ρi=∞; |λi|≤ρi/2 ou analogue?','The draft claims unbounded individual eigenvalue/singular-value ratios and asks for an index-shift pointwise bound.','Weyl gives |λ_n|≤(∏_(j≤n)s_j)^(1/n), not a universal same-index or fixed fractional-index bound. Finite cyclic weighted shifts with all eigenvalue moduli1 but all except one singular valueε disprove any fixed-index bound at index≥2. A compact block sum makes ratios unbounded.','claim plus explicit open question')
claim(84,420,'Normal Fredholm minors and resolvent bounds','K1-07','149–150','383–437','|Pn(u)|≤Pn(|u|); |R(u)|≤R(|u|)','For normal trace-class u, the nth Fredholm adjugate coefficient has at eigenvector i the elementary symmetric sum excluding λi. Hence |Pn(u)|≤Pn(|u|)≤e_n(|u|)I and ||R(u)||≤det(I+|u|). In infinite dimension positive u attains the last norm bound as eigenvalues tend to0.','The exclusion formula proves these statements, with alternating signs in Pn=Σ_(k=0)^n(−1)^k e_(n−k)(u)u^k. At a zero of det the entire adjugate is understood by continuation. The lower norm bound ||R(u)||≥|det(I+u)| uses infinite dimension and is not a general finite-dimensional statement.')
claim(85,420,'Claimed monotonicity of adjugate summands is false','K1-07','149','398–405','αn-k(u)u^k suite décroissante','The source claims the positive terms e_(n−k)(u)u^k decrease with k, and writes Pn as their unsigned sum.','For u=diag(2,1),n=2, the first terms are2I and3u, so the proposed order already fails. P2 is the alternating combination2I−3u+u²=0. The valid positivity and upper bound in claim084 follow from excluded-eigenvalue sums, not this false monotonicity.')
problem(6,'Trace minimax normalization and atoms',[22,23,24],'trace continue; preuve/prouver','Specify the ambient von Neumann algebra, faithful normal semifinite trace, unit-ball suprema and one-sided conventions.','Several early leaves are isolated fragments; point atoms in a C*-spectrum need not be continuous projections.')
problem(7,'The Non! annotation and absolute-value spectra',[27,28,29,70],'Non!; unitairement équivalents(?)','Preserve the unlocated marginal objection; distinguish nonzero singular values from zero eigenspace multiplicities.','The trace-class theorem is valid, but full unitary equivalence is false for a compact weighted shift.')
problem(8,'Exterior normalization and finite-rank verification',[31,32,35],'Ajouter préambule; vérifier d’après cas décomposable','Use Gram determinant normalization and verify rank-one coefficient1/n!.','The local embedding/quotient scale factors are reversed; the final norm estimate is valid.')
problem(9,'Spectral continuity placeholder',[33,76,81],'dans un sens évident à préciser; convergence des projecteurs spectraux','Use finite nonzero Riesz clusters and a summable matching topology with tails.','The topology is not stated; zero cannot be treated as an isolated spectral point in general.')
problem(10,'Nonnormal trace formula',[37,60,64],'Pour la preuve…ramènera au cas positif','Compare the analytic genus-zero reduction with the exact Hilbert-space Lidskii theorem.','The positive case alone cannot supply the nonnormal theorem; deleted and later proof fragments must be linked.')
problem(11,'Optimal singular-value sums',[38,39,40],'semblent les inégalités les plus fortes possibles','Compare sharpness of these determinant bounds with full Horn-type feasibility inequalities.','The source makes a tentative strongest-possible assertion without a proof.')
problem(12,'Subadditive functions of singular values',[41,42,43],'Question…forme générale des fonctions f; n arguments','State regularity/normalization assumptions and compare the concave sufficient class with the broader request.','A false stronger p-inequality precedes the genuine classification question.')
problem(13,'Convexity variants without symmetry',[45,47,49],'pas forcément croissante; sym deleted; ? near39','Equal determinant product removes monotonicity, not symmetry.','Explicit2×2 counterexamples refute the unsymmetric variants and the reversed reciprocal inequality.')
problem(14,'Duality and minimal/maximal completions',[51,52,53,54,55,56],'on peut le vérifier; total et fortement fermé?','Retain polar gauge and variance; distinguish finite-rank closure from maximal ideal.','Several summary lines omit exactly the polar or inclusion direction that the full proof supplies.')
problem(15,'Entire-function order and index errors',[58,59,60,61,62,63,64],'sauf erreur; genre1; Cauchy R^(n+1)','Use the reciprocal zero-density order, correct Cauchy exponent, and real logarithmic means.','The same dossier later corrects order1 to1/α, while other index/limsup slips remain.')
problem(16,'Entire holomorphy versus bounded type',[64,65],'pas une fonction entière','Interpret the everywhere-holomorphic example in modern Banach analysis and separate uniform Taylor radius.','Unboundedness on a bounded ball does not negate Fréchet holomorphy; the next margin itself calls S holomorphic.')
problem(17,'Mixed determinant inequality',[66,67,68],'|αn(ui)|≤αn(|ui|)','Retain the two-sided domain/range Cauchy–Schwarz product.','A rational2×2 counterexample rejects the one-sided statement while preserving determinant subadditivity.')
problem(18,'Analytic Hölder and noncommutative traces',[69,71,72,73,74,75],'dire quelles parties sont connues; généralisation; ?? r≥1','Separate elementary Hilbert-space proof from semifinite measurable-operator extensions and give exact trace assumptions.','The promised full-exponent proof is on the later leaves; its strict endpoint sign and missing lemmas need explicit treatment.')
problem(19,'Convergence and uniform convexity',[76,77,78],'Est-ce vrai si p<∞? Uniforme convexité?','Distinguish weak Banach convergence, WOT convergence, and quasi-Banach topologies for p≤1.','Uniform convexity answers1<p<∞, but it cannot answer p=1 or p<1.')
problem(20,'Fractional-power monotonicity',[77,79,80],'fonction croissante de r; Inégalités précises?','Test the full Schatten expression separately from the valid operator-norm Hölder estimate.','The manuscript conflates a one-singular-value norm bound with a sum over all singular values.')
problem(21,'Individual eigenvalue majorants',[82,83],'Majorations individuelles…ρi/2 ou analogue?','Use product/geometric-mean bounds; test proposed fixed index shifts on weighted cycles.','The leaf already anticipates failure of same-index control; the broader proposed shift requires a counterexample or theorem.')
problem(22,'Fredholm adjugate positivity',[84,85],'suite décroissante; Pn=Σαn-k u^k','Use alternating coefficients and the excluded-eigenvalue formula.','The claimed monotonicity of the unsigned terms fails, even though the stated final normal bounds are correct.')
